  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Thursday...\n\nConditions trend warmer today as ridging builds into the region,\nbringing high temperatures into the upper 80s to 90s for many\nlocations. While mostly dry conditions will prevail, some weak \nenergy moving in from the southwest may provide enough forcing to \ngenerate high-based showers and weak thunderstorms during the \nafternoon and evening (15 percent chance). With this, gusty and \nerratic winds may develop with dry air in place near the surface. \n\nThis weekend through Monday, there continues to be high \nconfidence in hot to very hot temperatures as a strong ridge of \nhigh pressure builds over the region. Ensembles suggest 500mb \nheights approaching 596-600 dm for Sunday. High temperatures of 95\nto 112 degrees are forecast, warmest on Sunday with very warm \ntemperatures aloft (700mb temperatures near or exceeding 20C by \nSunday and 850 mb temperatures of 35-40C). There continues to be a\nmoderate chance at exceeding 110 degrees for areas roughly from\nBillings east. Temperatures this warm could threaten all-time \nrecord high temperatures at all climate sites on Sunday. Along \nwith the hot daytime temperatures, overnight low temperatures in \nthe 60s and 70s, warmest Sunday night, will exacerbate the risk of\nheat stress.\n\nAlong with the heat, very dry conditions will exist, with \nrelative humidity values decreasing into the single digits to \nteens percent Saturday and Sunday, and teens to 20s percent \nMonday. Breezy south to southwest winds look to develop over \nwestern and central areas (gusts 20s to 30s mph) during the day on\nSunday, ahead of a cold front from the northwest Sunday \nevening/night. The combination of breezy winds, very hot \ntemperatures, and very dry conditions will increase fire weather\nconcerns. Take care not to cause a spark. \n\nHeading into Tuesday and the middle of next week, the ridge is \nprogged by ensembles to shift east a bit, allowing temperatures to\ncool slightly (highs in the 90s), and monsoonal moisture to move \nin from the southwest. This would allow for daily low to moderate\nshower/thunderstorm chances to return to the area along with\nhigher relative humidity values. Ensembles prog PWATs in excess of\nan inch for much of the area from Tuesday onward.\n\nBelow are the all-time record high temperatures at each of our \nclimate stations:\n\nBillings, MT    108F\nLivingston, MT  105F\nMiles City, MT  111F\nSheridan, WY    107F\nBaker, MT       109F\n\nSTP",
